.TH "TEMPORAL.PLAINDATE.PLAINDATE" "3JS" "September 18, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Temporal.PlainDate.PlainDate \- Temporal.PlainDate() constructor
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]Temporal.PlainDate()\f[B]\f[R] constructor creates
\f[CR]Temporal.PlainDate\f[R] objects.
.PP
This constructor allows you to create instances by directly supplying
the underlying data.
Like all other \f[CR]Temporal\f[R] classes, you should usually construct
\f[CR]Temporal.PlainDate\f[R] objects using the
\f[CR]Temporal.PlainDate.from()\f[R] static method, which can handle a
variety of input types.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
new Temporal.PlainDate(year, month, day)
new Temporal.PlainDate(year, month, day, calendar)
.EE
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] \f[CR]Temporal.PlainDate()\f[R] can only be constructed
with \f[CR]new\f[R].
Attempting to call it without \f[CR]new\f[R] throws a
\f[CR]TypeError\f[R].
.RE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]year\f[R]
A number, truncated to an integer, representing the year in the ISO
calendar system.
.TP
\f[B]month\f[R]
A number, truncated to an integer, representing the month in the ISO
calendar system.
.TP
\f[B]day\f[R]
A number, truncated to an integer, representing the day of the month in
the ISO calendar system.
.TP
\f[B]calendar\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A string representing the calendar to use.
See \f[CR]Intl.supportedValuesOf()\f[R] for a list of commonly supported
calendar types.
Defaults to \f[CR]\(dqiso8601\(dq\f[R].
Note that irrespective of the \f[CR]calendar\f[R], the \f[CR]year\f[R],
\f[CR]month\f[R], and \f[CR]day\f[R] must be in the ISO 8601 calendar
system.
.SS Return value
A new \f[CR]Temporal.PlainDate\f[R] object, representing the date
specified by \f[CR]year\f[R], \f[CR]month\f[R], \f[CR]day\f[R] (in the
ISO calendar), interpreted in the calendar system specified by
\f[CR]calendar\f[R].
.SS Exceptions
.TP
\f[B]TypeError\f[R]
Thrown if \f[CR]calendar\f[R] is not a string or \f[CR]undefined\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]RangeError\f[R]
Thrown in one of the following cases:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]year\f[R], \f[CR]month\f[R], or \f[CR]day\f[R] is not a finite
number.
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]year\f[R], \f[CR]month\f[R], and \f[CR]day\f[R] combination
does not represent a valid date in the ISO calendar system, or is not in
the representable range, which is ±(10\(ha8 + 1) days, or about
±273,972.6 years, from the Unix epoch.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]calendar\f[R] is not a valid calendar identifier.
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Using Temporal.PlainDate()
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] plainDate = \f[B]new\f[R] Temporal.PlainDate(2021, 7, 1);
console.log(plainDate.toString()); \f[I]// 2021\-07\-01\f[R]

\f[I]// Note that the date is stored internally as ISO 8601, even when it\(aqs\f[R]
\f[I]// interpreted in a different calendar system. For example, even though\f[R]
\f[I]// 2021\-07\-01 ISO is 5781\-10\-21 in the Hebrew calendar, you still pass the\f[R]
\f[I]// ISO date to the constructor.\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] plainDate2 = \f[B]new\f[R] Temporal.PlainDate(2021, 7, 1, \(dqhebrew\(dq);
console.log(plainDate2.toString()); \f[I]// 2021\-07\-01[u\-ca=hebrew]\f[R]
console.log(plainDate2.year); \f[I]// 5781\f[R]
console.log(plainDate2.month); \f[I]// 10\f[R]
console.log(plainDate2.day); \f[I]// 21\f[R]
.EE
